    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras are locked and loaded for a high-leverage slate, headlined by **Yordan Alvarez**, who anchors the offense with a massive 87.42 efficiency score. Facing Holmes, G, **Yordan Alvarez** benefits from a porous 4.77 xERA and an elite 0.444 xwOBA, positioning him as our undisputed primary threat for HR and RBI production. Complementing him is **Christian Encarnacion-Strand**, whose 69.90 score is bolstered significantly by a favorable park factor (+16%) and a exploitable matchup against Gasser. Meanwhile, **Pete Crow-Armstrong** earns a 69.85 rating; despite the inherent risk of a platoon disadvantage against Lodolo, his elite sprint speed and aggressive base-stealing outlook\u2014critical for our scarcity needs in SB\u2014make him a mandatory start. **Junior Caminero** rounds out our top tier with a 65.54 score, benefiting from the protection of the #3 hole and positive dynamic platoon splits against Bello.\n\nThe decision to bench **Chase Meidroth** was the tightest call on today\u2019s board. With a score of 45.22, he remained within the \"Marginal\" threshold but lacked the upside of our middle infield anchors **Trea Turner** and **Caleb Durbin**, whose stolen base utility and lineup positioning provided the necessary separation to secure their starts. We remain cautious of **Lars Nootbaar**, whose 50.83 score is heavily pressured by a matchup against Schlittler; while his lead-off role is an asset, the opposing pitcher\u2019s 2.88 xERA makes him our most vulnerable high-floor play. Looking ahead, we continue to monitor the status of **Christian Yelich** and **Hector Rodriguez**, both of whom remain sidelined, while keeping **Casey Schmitt**, **Pedro Ramirez**, **Luis Pena**, and **Jacob Gonzalez** in reserve for potential roster fluidity. Be advised that **Gary Sanchez** carries a minor reliance on recency bias, though his park-adjusted metrics should provide stable output in the bottom third of the order.",
